<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladimir II Monomakh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1053–<a href="/years/1125">1125</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Vladimir Monomakh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladimir Monomakh was Prince of Pereyaslavl and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and, from 1113, Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. A grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> and, through his mother, of the Byzantine emperor Constantine IX Monomachos, he led repeated campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and took part in the princely congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyubech">Lyubech</a> in 1097. His Instruction to his children is a classic of early Rus literature, and his sons ruled much of Rus after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh.jpg" alt="Vladimir II Monomakh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Печать Владимира II Мономаха · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%8C_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0_II_%D0%9C%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%85%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
